  "dom_id": "step-q114615",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/assets/09c3ef18cefb953df4469efa496bc8bdd57b0923c1a19842a8384b6d54ee78f1.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈1.5.$ So ${f}^{″}(1.5)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(1.5)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈1.5,$ the slope is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/assets/57e2244806cb3b1168da1a1be30f923b7ee721431addd926654fcb4e51c2063b.png)",
